[Assistant]: For the active set at $x^{*}$ put
\begin{equation*}
{\cal A}(x^{*})=\{i\;:\;g_{i}(x^{*})=0\}=\{1,2,3\},\qquad 
g_{4}(x^{*})=-1<0 .
\end{equation*}
In particular
\begin{equation*}
g_{1}(x)=(x_{1}-1)^{2}+x_{2}^{2}-1,\qquad 
g_{2}(x)=(x_{1}-3)^{2}+x_{2}^{2}-1,
\end{equation*}
so that on $F$
\begin{equation*}
(x_{1}-1)^{2}+x_{2}^{2}\le 1,\qquad (x_{1}-3)^{2}+x_{2}^{2}\le 1,
\end{equation*}
whence $(x_{1}-2)^{2}+2x_{2}^{2}\le 0$ and hence
\begin{equation*}
x_{1}=2,\qquad x_{2}=0 ,\qquad -2\le x_{3}\le -1,
\end{equation*}
on $F$.  In particular every $x^{(k)}\in F$ can be written
$x^{(k)}=(2,0,x_{3}^{(k)})$ with $x_{3}^{(k)}\in[-2,-1]$ and with
$x^{*}=(2,0,-1)$,
\begin{equation*}
\frac{x^{(k)}-x^{*}}{t^{(k)}}=
\Bigl(0,0,\frac{x_{3}^{(k)}+1}{t^{(k)}}\Bigr),\qquad t^{(k)}\searrow0,
\end{equation*}
so that
\begin{equation*}
T_{F}(x^{*})=\Bigl\{d=(d_{1},d_{2},d_{3})\in {\mathbb R}^{3}:
d_{1}=d_{2}=0,\;d_{3}\le 0\Bigr\}
     =\{0\}\times\{0\}\times{\mathbb R}_{-} ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. up to first order the feasible directions are the non-positive
directions in the $x_{3}$-axis.  In the linearised sense
\begin{equation*}
\nabla g_{1}(x^{*})=(2,0,0)^{t},\qquad
\nabla g_{2}(x^{*})=(-2,0,0)^{t},\qquad
\nabla g_{3}(x^{*})=(0,0,1)^{t},
\end{equation*}
so that the (regular) linearised tangent cone is
$T_{F}^{{\rm lin}}(x^{*})=\{d:\,2d_{1}\le0,\,-2d_{1}\le0,\,d_{3}\le0\}
=\{d_{1}=0,d_{3}\le0\}$.

The polar of the contingent cone is therefore
\begin{equation*}
T_{F}(x^{*})^{\circ}
   =\Bigl\{s=(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3})\in{\mathbb R}^{3}:
   s^{t}d\le 0\;\;\forall\,d\in T_{F}(x^{*})\Bigr\}
   =\Bigl\{s:\;s_{3}d_{3}\le0\;\forall d_{3}\le0\Bigr\},
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the usual notation
\begin{equation*}
T_{F}^{\circ}(x^{*})={\mathbb R}^{2}\times{\mathbb R}_{+}
      =\{(s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}):s_{3}\ge 0\},
\end{equation*}
and $s^{t}d=s_{3}d_{3}\le0$ for every $d\in T_{F}(x^{*})$.  In
particular $s_{3}\ge0$ is the only restriction coming from the
contingent cone; $s_{1},s_{2}$ are free in the polar sense.  Up to the
usual $s_{2}$–free identification this is the familiar Lagrange
description

\[
N^{\rm reg}_{F}(x^{*})=\Bigl\{\sum_{i\in{\cal A}(x^{*})}
\lambda_{i}\nabla g_{i}(x^{*}):\lambda_{i}\ge0\Bigr\},
\]

i.e. with $\lambda_{i}\ge0$, $\lambda_{i}g_{i}(x^{*})=0$,
\begin{equation*}
s=\lambda_{1}\nabla g_{1}(x^{*})+\lambda_{2}\nabla g_{2}(x^{*})
   +\lambda_{3}\nabla g_{3}(x^{*})
   =\bigl(2\lambda_{1}-2\lambda_{2},\,0,\,\lambda_{3}\bigr),
\qquad \lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\lambda_{3}\ge0,
\end{equation*}
which is
\[
N^{\rm reg}_{F}(x^{*})=\{(s_{1},0,s_{3})\in{\mathbb R}^{3}:s_{3}\ge0\}
      ={\mathbb R}\times\{0\}\times{\mathbb R}_{+},
\]
the cone generated by $\nabla g_{1}(x^{*})$, $\nabla g_{2}(x^{*})$ and
$\nabla g_{3}(x^{*})$.  The $s_{2}$-component is free in the
contingent polar $T_{F}(x^{*})^{\circ}={\mathbb R}^{2}\times{\mathbb R}_{+}$;
the regular Lagrange normal cone is the usual $s_{2}=0$ section above.
This is the customary description $N_{F}(x^{*})= {\rm cone}\{\nabla
g_{i}(x^{*}),i\in{\cal A}(x^{*})\}$, the non-regularity $d_{2}\neq0$
being killed by the curvature of $g_{1},g_{2}$ at $x^{*}$.  Removing the
inessential $s_{2}$-freedom this is often written simply as
$s=(s_{1},s_{3})$ with $s_{3}\ge0$, i.e. the normal directions are the
non-negative multiples of $e_{3}$ together with the whole $e_{1}$-line,
the usual limiting normal cone at the non-regular point $x^{*}$.
